## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to implement a compact ranking structure with duplicates and a fixed value range, using O(log U) updates and O(log U) kth-order queries.

## Key Concepts

- Fenwick tree for prefix sums
- Binary lifting to find k-th order statistic (standard BIT trick: find largest power of 2 <= n, then iterate downward checking cumulative tree values)
- Handling duplicates via per-value counts

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n) { 
    this.n = n; 
    this.tree = new Array(n + 1).fill(0); 
  }

  add(i, delta) {
    for (; i <= this.n; i += i & -i) this.tree[i] += delta;
  }

  sum(i) {
    let s = 0;
    for (; i > 0; i -= i & -i) s += this.tree[i];
    return s;
  }

  kth(k) {
    if (k < 1) return -1;
    const total = this.sum(this.n);
    if (k > total) return -1;

    let idx = 0;
    let bit = 1;
    while (bit << 1 <= this.n) bit <<= 1;

    let sum = 0;
    for (; bit > 0; bit >>= 1) {
      const next = idx + bit;
      if (next <= this.n && sum + this.tree[next] < k) {
        sum += this.tree[next];
        idx = next;
      }
    }
    return idx; // 0-indexed
  }
}

class Leaderboard {
  constructor(maxScore = 1000000) {
    this.bit = new Fenwick(maxScore + 1);
  }

  insertScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, 1); }

  eraseScore(s) { this.bit.add(s + 1, -1); }

  getKthScore(k) { return this.bit.kth(k) - 1; }

  getRank(s) { return this.bit.sum(s + 1); }
}
```

## Handling Ties

Since each score has its own count, duplicates naturally work:
- insertScore(50) adds count at index 50
- insertScore(50) again increments same index
- eraseScore(50) decrements it
- getRank(50) returns all scores <= 50 including duplicates
- getKthScore(k) skips to the k-th element accounting for counts

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time analytics require fast percentile queries with inserts/deletes while handling duplicates.

## Key Concepts
- Coordinate compression maps latencies to a compact domain.
- Fenwick tree maintains prefix counts efficiently.
- kth-order statistic via binary lifting yields the percentile value.
- Carefully handle erase when a value count reaches zero.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.bit=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  update(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.bit[i]+=delta; }
  query(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.bit[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){ let idx=0; let bit=1; while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit<<=1; for(; bit>0; bit>>=1){ let nxt = idx + bit; if(nxt <= this.n && this.bit[nxt] < k){ idx = nxt; k -= this.bit[nxt]; } } return idx+1; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle unseen latency values without rebuilding the compression map?
- Compare this approach to a wavelet tree for large domains or streaming data.

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates knowledge of practical data-structure trade-offs for dynamic ranking. Candidates should justify when to sort on demand vs. maintain auxiliary structures.

## Key Concepts
- Hash map for O(1) key access
- On-demand sorting for top-k retrieval
- Trade-off: simpler updates vs. slower top-k retrieval

## Code Example
```javascript
class CounterTopK {
  constructor() {
    this.counts = new Map(); // key -> count
  }
  add(key, delta) {
    const cur = this.counts.get(key) || 0;
    const next = cur + delta;
    if (next <= 0) this.counts.delete(key); else this.counts.set(key, next);
  }
  get(key) { return this.counts.get(key) || 0; }
  topK(k) {
    const arr = Array.from(this.counts.entries());
    arr.sort((a,b)=> b[1]-a[1] || a[0].localeCompare(b[0]));
    return arr.slice(0,k).map(([key]) => key);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
